{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAzBneuzQ7zvwQAK1oht","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019y5PiK6oeL9NgZWtpNup86","name":"mcp__ac_review__source_read","input":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-442","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[442,500]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2236,"cache_read_input_tokens":158343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2236},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"8850d378-86fd-4f5f-923a-0e6dbb12747d","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:15.418Z","request_id":"req_011CgAzBnGMoyMD9rdaZEAvS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019y5PiK6oeL9NgZWtpNup86":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-442","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[442,500]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019y5PiK6oeL9NgZWtpNup86","display_name":"Source Read","server_display_name":"a_c.graphify.fd-support","tool_name":"source.read"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019y5PiK6oeL9NgZWtpNup86","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:e430a7bc686d520fca34115df15c6981c10be2665473223130cc3d69e07f8d0a\",\"sha256\":\"e430a7bc686d520fca34115df15c6981c10be2665473223130cc3d69e07f8d0a\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-442\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=a94090adefcc2a02c9de34dff2610fabcbb1ec2bb8d66ad7495ab7b80b6b1541]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 5. Ontology-first design\\n\\nEvery new or materially changed active semantic boundary derives its\\nsemantic model from one accepted Ontology (P1, P6;\\nrc.2 #4b-ontology-first-design-rule). Before a new or changed entity, function,\\nauthority, effect, Prime-conformant carrier, module boundary, public\\noperation or schema is promoted, the affected boundary first produces a\\ncandidate Ontology slice and carries it through the acceptance sequence\\nbelow.\\n\\nThe Ontology is the prior semantic-design authority for the boundary. It\\nis subordinate to the constitutional WHAT: it derives from intent,\\nproduct, requirements and the applicable graph or domain profile, and it\\ndoes not invent product meaning. Once accepted, it governs semantic\\nentities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, functions,\\ncomposition and effects (P6, P2). It does not replace target architecture\\nor module design: accepted design remains the structural HOW for topology,\\ninterfaces, carrier placement, module boundaries, algorithms and local\\nrealisation. Implementation and tests derive from requirements plus\\naccepted design and preserve the accepted Ontology; they are not direct\\nsubstitutes for either layer (P6; SPEC_METHOD §2 Constitutional chain).\\n\\nOntology acceptance identifies the constitutional sources it derives from\\nand the design authority that accepted it. It carries stable element and\\nrelation identities plus a version or basis that makes source change,\\nstaleness and the affected projection set decidable (P3; FOUNDATIONS §2,\\nBasis). A reprice of a governing source invalidates only the affected\\nOntology relations and downstream projections until their impact is\\nre-evaluated; the reprice is an instance of the material input change of\\n[ACTIVATION §7](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse), which owns the\\ninvalidators. Code, tests, generated schemas or a single implementing\\nagent cannot self-ratify missing Ontology authority (P6). The Ontology\\nverdict may live in the same design pack and acceptance record as the\\nthree-view verdict (§7); this rule does not require a second document,\\nticket or approval ceremony.\\n\\nProportionality applies (ACTIVATION §6). A material semantic boundary is\\none whose change affects identity, authority, lifecycle, public contract,\\nexternally observable Product meaning, cross-module topology, or accepted\\neffect or closure law. A `realization_refactor` wholly inside an accepted\\nboundary may cite the existing Ontology and design basis and prove no\\nOntology delta; it does not require re-authoring or re-accepting unchanged\\nevidence. A semantic or structural change publishes only the affected\\nOntology and design delta and its projection impact set. Unaffected\\nmaintenance and unrelated boundaries do not freeze merely because one\\nsource digest changed (P2, P0).\\n\\nFor this profile (P1):\\n\\n```text\\nOntology =\\n  entities + identities + relationships + invariants + lifecycle\\n  + authority + atomic functions + higher-order composition\\n  + effects + projections\\n```\\n\\nA class inventory, schema list, endpoint list, command list or carrier\\ncensus alone is not an Ontology (P1).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[442,500]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"f499476ba3ae858fecad437f37b66e4fdfd65f0b33ce74c9bd670751c33c09b4\",\"size_bytes\":3115,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[442,500]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"ed0135cc-41c6-40c1-a1f2-ce969bb51c76","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:15.580Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:e430a7bc686d520fca34115df15c6981c10be2665473223130cc3d69e07f8d0a\",\"sha256\":\"e430a7bc686d520fca34115df15c6981c10be2665473223130cc3d69e07f8d0a\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-442\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=a94090adefcc2a02c9de34dff2610fabcbb1ec2bb8d66ad7495ab7b80b6b1541]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 5. Ontology-first design\\n\\nEvery new or materially changed active semantic boundary derives its\\nsemantic model from one accepted Ontology (P1, P6;\\nrc.2 #4b-ontology-first-design-rule). Before a new or changed entity, function,\\nauthority, effect, Prime-conformant carrier, module boundary, public\\noperation or schema is promoted, the affected boundary first produces a\\ncandidate Ontology slice and carries it through the acceptance sequence\\nbelow.\\n\\nThe Ontology is the prior semantic-design authority for the boundary. It\\nis subordinate to the constitutional WHAT: it derives from intent,\\nproduct, requirements and the applicable graph or domain profile, and it\\ndoes not invent product meaning. Once accepted, it governs semantic\\nentities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, functions,\\ncomposition and effects (P6, P2). It does not replace target architecture\\nor module design: accepted design remains the structural HOW for topology,\\ninterfaces, carrier placement, module boundaries, algorithms and local\\nrealisation. Implementation and tests derive from requirements plus\\naccepted design and preserve the accepted Ontology; they are not direct\\nsubstitutes for either layer (P6; SPEC_METHOD §2 Constitutional chain).\\n\\nOntology acceptance identifies the constitutional sources it derives from\\nand the design authority that accepted it. It carries stable element and\\nrelation identities plus a version or basis that makes source change,\\nstaleness and the affected projection set decidable (P3; FOUNDATIONS §2,\\nBasis). A reprice of a governing source invalidates only the affected\\nOntology relations and downstream projections until their impact is\\nre-evaluated; the reprice is an instance of the material input change of\\n[ACTIVATION §7](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse), which owns the\\ninvalidators. Code, tests, generated schemas or a single implementing\\nagent cannot self-ratify missing Ontology authority (P6). The Ontology\\nverdict may live in the same design pack and acceptance record as the\\nthree-view verdict (§7); this rule does not require a second document,\\nticket or approval ceremony.\\n\\nProportionality applies (ACTIVATION §6). A material semantic boundary is\\none whose change affects identity, authority, lifecycle, public contract,\\nexternally observable Product meaning, cross-module topology, or accepted\\neffect or closure law. A `realization_refactor` wholly inside an accepted\\nboundary may cite the existing Ontology and design basis and prove no\\nOntology delta; it does not require re-authoring or re-accepting unchanged\\nevidence. A semantic or structural change publishes only the affected\\nOntology and design delta and its projection impact set. Unaffected\\nmaintenance and unrelated boundaries do not freeze merely because one\\nsource digest changed (P2, P0).\\n\\nFor this profile (P1):\\n\\n```text\\nOntology =\\n  entities + identities + relationships + invariants + lifecycle\\n  + authority + atomic functions + higher-order composition\\n  + effects + projections\\n```\\n\\nA class inventory, schema list, endpoint list, command list or carrier\\ncensus alone is not an Ontology (P1).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[442,500]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"f499476ba3ae858fecad437f37b66e4fdfd65f0b33ce74c9bd670751c33c09b4\",\"size_bytes\":3115,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[442,500]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}","structuredContent":{"evidence":{"id":"sha256:e430a7bc686d520fca34115df15c6981c10be2665473223130cc3d69e07f8d0a","sha256":"e430a7bc686d520fca34115df15c6981c10be2665473223130cc3d69e07f8d0a"},"id":"p2-read-dm-442","operation":"source.read","operation_outcome":{"cause":"declared output available","core_value":"satisfied","evidence":[],"limits":["output availability only; no semantic acceptance"],"predicate":"B(source.read)[request_sha256=a94090adefcc2a02c9de34dff2610fabcbb1ec2bb8d66ad7495ab7b80b6b1541]: declared output availability","source_parent":{"clause":"results","member":"FOUNDATIONS.md","sha256":"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f"},"value":"returned"},"refusal":null,"result":{"artifact":null,"value":{"acquisition":{},"content":"## 5. Ontology-first design\n\nEvery new or materially changed active semantic boundary derives its\nsemantic model from one accepted Ontology (P1, P6;\nrc.2 #4b-ontology-first-design-rule). Before a new or changed entity, function,\nauthority, effect, Prime-conformant carrier, module boundary, public\noperation or schema is promoted, the affected boundary first produces a\ncandidate Ontology slice and carries it through the acceptance sequence\nbelow.\n\nThe Ontology is the prior semantic-design authority for the boundary. It\nis subordinate to the constitutional WHAT: it derives from intent,\nproduct, requirements and the applicable graph or domain profile, and it\ndoes not invent product meaning. Once accepted, it governs semantic\nentities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, functions,\ncomposition and effects (P6, P2). It does not replace target architecture\nor module design: accepted design remains the structural HOW for topology,\ninterfaces, carrier placement, module boundaries, algorithms and local\nrealisation. Implementation and tests derive from requirements plus\naccepted design and preserve the accepted Ontology; they are not direct\nsubstitutes for either layer (P6; SPEC_METHOD §2 Constitutional chain).\n\nOntology acceptance identifies the constitutional sources it derives from\nand the design authority that accepted it. It carries stable element and\nrelation identities plus a version or basis that makes source change,\nstaleness and the affected projection set decidable (P3; FOUNDATIONS §2,\nBasis). A reprice of a governing source invalidates only the affected\nOntology relations and downstream projections until their impact is\nre-evaluated; the reprice is an instance of the material input change of\n[ACTIVATION §7](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse), which owns the\ninvalidators. Code, tests, generated schemas or a single implementing\nagent cannot self-ratify missing Ontology authority (P6). The Ontology\nverdict may live in the same design pack and acceptance record as the\nthree-view verdict (§7); this rule does not require a second document,\nticket or approval ceremony.\n\nProportionality applies (ACTIVATION §6). A material semantic boundary is\none whose change affects identity, authority, lifecycle, public contract,\nexternally observable Product meaning, cross-module topology, or accepted\neffect or closure law. A `realization_refactor` wholly inside an accepted\nboundary may cite the existing Ontology and design basis and prove no\nOntology delta; it does not require re-authoring or re-accepting unchanged\nevidence. A semantic or structural change publishes only the affected\nOntology and design delta and its projection impact set. Unaffected\nmaintenance and unrelated boundaries do not freeze merely because one\nsource digest changed (P2, P0).\n\nFor this profile (P1):\n\n```text\nOntology =\n  entities + identities + relationships + invariants + lifecycle\n  + authority + atomic functions + higher-order composition\n  + effects + projections\n```\n\nA class inventory, schema list, endpoint list, command list or carrier\ncensus alone is not an Ontology (P1).\n\n","coordinates":{"lines":[442,500]},"encoding":"utf8","parent_sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220","selected_sha256":"f499476ba3ae858fecad437f37b66e4fdfd65f0b33ce74c9bd670751c33c09b4","size_bytes":3115,"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[442,500]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"}}},"schema":"a_c.fd-support.response/1","status":"ok"}},"tool_result_meta":[{"i