{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAzBneuzQ7zvwQAK1oht","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FRvbURnCGTWXKJbDpQTZDn","name":"mcp__ac_review__source_read","input":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-572","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[572,616]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2236,"cache_read_input_tokens":158343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2236},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"1de2b5fd-3bee-4781-a1ed-4ed12e97c164","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:18.114Z","request_id":"req_011CgAzBnGMoyMD9rdaZEAvS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FRvbURnCGTWXKJbDpQTZDn":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-572","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[572,616]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FRvbURnCGTWXKJbDpQTZDn","display_name":"Source Read","server_display_name":"a_c.graphify.fd-support","tool_name":"source.read"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRvbURnCGTWXKJbDpQTZDn","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:3a4e085fe0eda5bc4316d4d1fd8713540774a93760227995e6d77bd9cf0accf0\",\"sha256\":\"3a4e085fe0eda5bc4316d4d1fd8713540774a93760227995e6d77bd9cf0accf0\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-572\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=739ef530f46fee79edd34498d15bb8c04e501171400f5b2918b2063bd7ae40d8]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"### Decision-complete symbolic design\\n\\nFor one governed boundary `B`, the complete material-relation set is (P1,\\nP2; rc.2 #decision-complete-symbolic-design):\\n\\n```text\\nM(B) =\\n  entities + identities + relationships + cardinalities + invariants\\n  + authorities + authority transitions + functions\\n  + higher-order composition laws + topology\\n  + lifecycle states and transitions + public contracts\\n  + externally observable Product meaning\\n  + declared domains and admission conditions\\n  + failure and refusal routes + effect and closure laws\\n  + projections + module mappings + material algorithmic obligations\\n```\\n\\nA design boundary is decision-complete when `U(B)` is empty, `M(B)` forms\\none jointly satisfiable constraint network, and every network admitted by\\n`M(B)` is semantically equivalent under the governing Product,\\nrequirements and accepted design relations. This is the design gate's\\nacceptance criterion: a boundary is ready for retained implementation\\nwithout a prior gate exactly when it is decision-complete, and a gate that\\ncannot reach decision completeness has not closed (P2, P6).\\n\\nOntology, Prime contraction, IACS, target design, the domain, sequence and\\nstate views, public contracts, module boundaries, implementation and tests\\nare projections of that one network at different reasoning altitudes.\\nTheir presence, section names, individual plausibility or mechanical\\nvalidity does not establish decision completeness (P1, EVALUATION §1).\\n\\nThe boundary is underconstrained whenever `decision_complete(B)` is false,\\nincluding when implementation has to introduce a new material semantic\\ndecision, projections contradict one another, or two materially\\nnon-equivalent networks remain admissible. Local algorithm and layout\\nchoices may remain open only when every admitted choice is semantically\\nequivalent under the accepted network. Contradictory projections make the\\ndesign unsatisfiable and block promotion (P1, P5): an unsatisfiable design\\nfails the design gate's acceptance criterion, so this block is a\\nprecondition of the gate (§7 Gate and retrospective work), not a decision\\non a review finding. Decision completeness\\ndoes not require another document: the constraint network may be expressed\\nthrough the existing Ontology, algebra, module mapping and views when those\\nsurfaces jointly carry the complete relation (P2).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[572,616]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"7a7c7bcb7b20420cc92a368501c8a8aa4d42ba523a911451f664f1de24231be5\",\"size_bytes\":2352,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[572,616]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"f977260f-f78a-45e9-99a6-86a4268ffe92","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:18.128Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:3a4e085fe0eda5bc4316d4d1fd8713540774a93760227995e6d77bd9cf0accf0\",\"sha256\":\"3a4e085fe0eda5bc4316d4d1fd8713540774a93760227995e6d77bd9cf0accf0\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-572\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=739ef530f46fee79edd34498d15bb8c04e501171400f5b2918b2063bd7ae40d8]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"### Decision-complete symbolic design\\n\\nFor one governed boundary `B`, the complete material-relation set is (P1,\\nP2; rc.2 #decision-complete-symbolic-design):\\n\\n```text\\nM(B) =\\n  entities + identities + relationships + cardinalities + invariants\\n  + authorities + authority transitions + functions\\n  + higher-order composition laws + topology\\n  + lifecycle states and transitions + public contracts\\n  + externally observable Product meaning\\n  + declared domains and admission conditions\\n  + failure and refusal routes + effect and closure laws\\n  + projections + module mappings + material algorithmic obligations\\n```\\n\\nA design boundary is decision-complete when `U(B)` is empty, `M(B)` forms\\none jointly satisfiable constraint network, and every network admitted by\\n`M(B)` is semantically equivalent under the governing Product,\\nrequirements and accepted design relations. This is the design gate's\\nacceptance criterion: a boundary is ready for retained implementation\\nwithout a prior gate exactly when it is decision-complete, and a gate that\\ncannot reach decision completeness has not closed (P2, P6).\\n\\nOntology, Prime contraction, IACS, target design, the domain, sequence and\\nstate views, public contracts, module boundaries, implementation and tests\\nare projections of that one network at different reasoning altitudes.\\nTheir presence, section names, individual plausibility or mechanical\\nvalidity does not establish decision completeness (P1, EVALUATION §1).\\n\\nThe boundary is underconstrained whenever `decision_complete(B)` is false,\\nincluding when implementation has to introduce a new material semantic\\ndecision, projections contradict one another, or two materially\\nnon-equivalent networks remain admissible. Local algorithm and layout\\nchoices may remain open only when every admitted choice is semantically\\nequivalent under the accepted network. Contradictory projections make the\\ndesign unsatisfiable and block promotion (P1, P5): an unsatisfiable design\\nfails the design gate's acceptance criterion, so this block is a\\nprecondition of the gate (§7 Gate and retrospective work), not a decision\\non a review finding. Decision completeness\\ndoes not require another document: the constraint network may be expressed\\nthrough the existing Ontology, algebra, module mapping and views when those\\nsurfaces jointly carry the complete relation (P2).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[572,616]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"7a7c7bcb7b20420cc92a368501c8a8aa4d42ba523a911451f664f1de24231be5\",\"size_bytes\":2352,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[572,616]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}","structuredContent":{"evidence":{"id":"sha256:3a4e085fe0eda5bc4316d4d1fd8713540774a93760227995e6d77bd9cf0accf0","sha256":"3a4e085fe0eda5bc4316d4d1fd8713540774a93760227995e6d77bd9cf0accf0"},"id":"p2-read-dm-572","operation":"source.read","operation_outcome":{"cause":"declared output available","core_value":"satisfied","evidence":[],"limits":["output availability only; no semantic acceptance"],"predicate":"B(source.read)[request_sha256=739ef530f46fee79edd34498d15bb8c04e501171400f5b2918b2063bd7ae40d8]: declared output availability","source_parent":{"clause":"results","member":"FOUNDATIONS.md","sha256":"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f"},"value":"returned"},"refusal":null,"result":{"artifact":null,"value":{"acquisition":{},"content":"### Decision-complete symbolic design\n\nFor one governed boundary `B`, the complete material-relation set is (P1,\nP2; rc.2 #decision-complete-symbolic-design):\n\n```text\nM(B) =\n  entities + identities + relationships + cardinalities + invariants\n  + authorities + authority transitions + functions\n  + higher-order composition laws + topology\n  + lifecycle states and transitions + public contracts\n  + externally observable Product meaning\n  + declared domains and admission conditions\n  + failure and refusal routes + effect and closure laws\n  + projections + module mappings + material algorithmic obligations\n```\n\nA design boundary is decision-complete when `U(B)` is empty, `M(B)` forms\none jointly satisfiable constraint network, and every network admitted by\n`M(B)` is semantically equivalent under the governing Product,\nrequirements and accepted design relations. This is the design gate's\nacceptance criterion: a boundary is ready for retained implementation\nwithout a prior gate exactly when it is decision-complete, and a gate that\ncannot reach decision completeness has not closed (P2, P6).\n\nOntology, Prime contraction, IACS, target design, the domain, sequence and\nstate views, public contracts, module boundaries, implementation and tests\nare projections of that one network at different reasoning altitudes.\nTheir presence, section names, individual plausibility or mechanical\nvalidity does not establish decision completeness (P1, EVALUATION §1).\n\nThe boundary is underconstrained whenever `decision_complete(B)` is false,\nincluding when implementation has to introduce a new material semantic\ndecision, projections contradict one another, or two materially\nnon-equivalent networks remain admissible. Local algorithm and layout\nchoices may remain open only when every admitted choice is semantically\nequivalent under the accepted network. Contradictory projections make the\ndesign unsatisfiable and block promotion (P1, P5): an unsatisfiable design\nfails the design gate's acceptance criterion, so this block is a\nprecondition of the gate (§7 Gate and retrospective work), not a decision\non a review finding. Decision completeness\ndoes not require another document: the constraint network may be expressed\nthrough the existing Ontology, algebra, module mapping and views when those\nsurfaces jointly carry the complete relation (P2).\n\n","coordinates":{"lines":[572,616]},"encoding":"utf8","parent_sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220","selected_sha256":"7a7c7bcb7b20420cc92a368501c8a8aa4d42ba523a911451f664f1de24231be5","size_bytes":2352,"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[572,616]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"}}},"schema":"a_c.fd-support.response/1","status":"ok"}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FRvbURnCGTWXKJbDpQTZDn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAzBneuzQ7zvwQAK1oht","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KHXeax9EEd8KtJQ3pxBcYD","name":"mcp__ac_review__source_read","input":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-1312","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1312,1354]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2236,"cache_read_input_tokens":158343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2236},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"523d8c25-a2af-4804-a020-4c5c27b5b316","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:20.523Z","request_id":"req_011CgAzBnGMoyMD9rdaZEAvS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KHXeax9EEd8KtJQ3pxBcYD":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-1312","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1312,1354]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KHXeax9EEd8KtJQ3pxBcYD","display_name":"Source Read","server_display_name":"a_c.graphify.fd-support","tool_name":"source.read"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KHXeax9EEd8KtJQ3pxBcYD","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:cf714bc76fae1f58efa633c20d9dd0bf03b726b4ee6142b2db620559b9346d3a\",\"sha256\":\"cf714bc76fae1f58efa633c20d9dd0bf03b726b4ee6142b2db620559b9346d3a\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-1312\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=f9a8ca235d06d17cb7a255f7800538a9fd65d21ac01ae6d4b9f6c4aba16555a8]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 7. Ontology and three-view design gate\\n\\nEvery new or materially changed semantic or typed module boundary carries\\none accepted Ontology and one complete three-view Mermaid design asset\\nbefore the boundary is promoted or claims design-method closure (P6, P1;\\nrc.2 #5e-ontology-and-three-view-behavioral-design-gate). When\\n`co_evolution_admissible(B)` holds, design evidence, implementation and\\ntests may develop together before that gate; otherwise the affected\\nunresolved, contradictory or materially non-equivalent relation is\\naccepted before retained implementation establishes it. Unchanged\\nboundaries cite their accepted basis; local realisation work with no\\nmaterial semantic-boundary delta does not recreate this gate (P2).\\n\\nThe asset contains all three views: a Mermaid `classDiagram` domain model;\\na Mermaid `sequenceDiagram` execution model; and a Mermaid\\n`stateDiagram-v2` lifecycle model. A project may ratify another text-native\\ndiagram format only when it preserves the same three distinct views and the\\nsame cross-view checks. One broad flowchart does not substitute for the\\nthree models. The three views may be sections of one existing design\\nsurface and may share one acceptance decision; this rule does not require\\nthree files, three tickets, three reviews or three approval ceremonies\\n(P2). The views are not decorative and are not peer truth surfaces: they\\nare fidelity-checked projections of the accepted Ontology, and together\\nthe sign-off surface for domain identity, authority, behaviour, lifecycle\\nand axiom conformance (P1, P6).\\n\\n### Ontology evidence\\n\\nThe design asset cites one accepted Ontology for the active boundary and\\nincludes or references its (P6; rc.2 #ontology-evidence): entity and\\nrelationship inventory; invariant and cardinality law; entity-lifecycle\\ncompleteness matrix; authority matrix; atomic-function and\\nhigher-order-function derivation matrix; governing composition and effect\\nalgebra where applicable; whole-family Prime contraction, including every\\naccepted atom's admitted domain and realisation-projection mapping; and\\nexplicit deferred, excluded and unresolved functionality.\\n\\nThe Ontology may be a boundary-bounded slice of a wider accepted product\\nOntology. The slice cites its parent and preserves parent identity,\\nrelationship, lifecycle, authority and function law. Copying selected rows\\ninto a new local model without that derivation creates another truth\\nsurface (P6, P3).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[1312,1354]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"4dd74835bab9775209a769aa0b59378bbb579294e528c56ba001600da829fd76\",\"size_bytes\":2437,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[1312,1354]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"90b842e4-0797-4899-a390-7c1d09b8d932","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:20.535Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:cf714bc76fae1f58efa633c20d9dd0bf03b726b4ee6142b2db620559b9346d3a\",\"sha256\":\"cf714bc76fae1f58efa633c20d9dd0bf03b726b4ee6142b2db620559b9346d3a\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-1312\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=f9a8ca235d06d17cb7a255f7800538a9fd65d21ac01ae6d4b9f6c4aba16555a8]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 7. Ontology and three-view design gate\\n\\nEvery new or materially changed semantic or typed module boundary carries\\none accepted Ontology and one complete three-view Mermaid design asset\\nbefore the boundary is promoted or claims design-method closure (P6, P1;\\nrc.2 #5e-ontology-and-three-view-behavioral-design-gate). When\\n`co_evolution_admissible(B)` holds, design evidence, implementation and\\ntests may develop together before that gate; otherwise the affected\\nunresolved, contradictory or materially non-equivalent relation is\\naccepted before retained implementation establishes it. Unchanged\\nboundaries cite their accepted basis; local realisation work with no\\nmaterial semantic-boundary delta does not recreate this gate (P2).\\n\\nThe asset contains all three views: a Mermaid `classDiagram` domain model;\\na Mermaid `sequenceDiagram` execution model; and a Mermaid\\n`stateDiagram-v2` lifecycle model. A project may ratify another text-native\\ndiagram format only when it preserves the same three distinct views and the\\nsame cross-view checks. One broad flowchart does not substitute for the\\nthree models. The three views may be sections of one existing design\\nsurface and may share one acceptance decision; this rule does not require\\nthree files, three tickets, three reviews or three approval ceremonies\\n(P2). The views are not decorative and are not peer truth surfaces: they\\nare fidelity-checked projections of the accepted Ontology, and together\\nthe sign-off surface for domain identity, authority, behaviour, lifecycle\\nand axiom conformance (P1, P6).\\n\\n### Ontology evidence\\n\\nThe design asset cites one accepted Ontology for the active boundary and\\nincludes or references its (P6; rc.2 #ontology-evidence): entity and\\nrelationship inventory; invariant and cardinality law; entity-lifecycle\\ncompleteness matrix; authority matrix; atomic-function and\\nhigher-order-function derivation matrix; governing composition and effect\\nalgebra where applicable; whole-family Prime contraction, including every\\naccepted atom's admitted domain and realisation-projection mapping; and\\nexplicit deferred, excluded and unresolved functionality.\\n\\nThe Ontology may be a boundary-bounded slice of a wider accepted product\\nOntology. The slice cites its parent and preserves parent identity,\\nrelationship, lifecycle, authority and function law. Copying selected rows\\ninto a new local model without that derivation creates another truth\\nsurface (P6, P3).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[1312,1354]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"4dd74835bab9775209a769aa0b59378bbb579294e528c56ba001600da829fd76\",\"size_bytes\":2437,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[1312,1354]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}","structuredContent":{"evidence":{"id":"sha256:cf714bc76fae1f58efa633c20d9dd0bf03b726b4ee6142b2db620559b9346d3a","sha256":"cf714bc76fae1f58efa633c20d9dd0bf03b726b4ee6142b2db620559b9346d3a"},"id":"p2-read-dm-1312","operation":"source.read","operation_outcome":{"cause":"declared output available","core_value":"satisfied","evidence":[],"limits":["output availability only; no semantic acceptance"],"predicate":"B(source.read)[request_sha256=f9a8ca235d06d17cb7a255f7800538a9fd65d21ac01ae6d4b9f6c4aba16555a8]: declared output availability","source_parent":{"clause":"results","member":"FOUNDATIONS.md","sha256":"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f"},"value":"returned"},"refusal":null,"result":{"artifact":null,"value":{"acquisition":{},"content":"## 7. Ontology and three-view design gate\n\nEvery new or materially changed semantic or typed module boundary carries\none accepted Ontology and one complete three-view Mermaid design asset\nbefore the boundary is promoted or claims design-method closure (P6, P1;\nrc.2 #5e-ontology-and-three-view-behavioral-design-gate). When\n`co_evolution_admissible(B)` holds, design evidence, implementation and\ntests may develop together before that gate; otherwise the affected\nunresolved, contradictory or materially non-equivalent relation is\naccepted before retained implementation establishes it. Unchanged\nboundaries cite their accepted basis; local realisation work with no\nmaterial semantic-boundary delta does not recreate this gate (P2).\n\nThe asset contains all three views: a Mermaid `classDiagram` domain model;\na Mermaid `sequenceDiagram` execution model; and a Mermaid\n`stateDiagram-v2` lifecycle model. A project may ratify another text-native\ndiagram format only when it preserves the same three distinct views and the\nsame cross-view checks. One broad flowchart does not substitute for the\nthree models. The three views may be sections of one existing design\nsurface and may share one acceptance decision; this rule does not require\nthree files, three tickets, three reviews or three approval ceremonies\n(P2). The views are not decorative and are not peer truth surfaces: they\nare fidelity-checked projections of the accepted Ontology, and together\nthe sign-off surface for domain identity, authority, behaviour, lifecycle\nand axiom conformance (P1, P6).\n\n### Ontology evidence\n\nThe design asset cites one accepted Ontology for the active boundary and\nincludes or references its (P6; rc.2 #ontology-evidence): entity and\nrelationship inventory; invariant and cardinality law; entity-lifecycle\ncompleteness matrix; authority matrix; atomic-function and\nhigher-order-function derivation matrix; governing composition and effect\nalgebra where applicable; whole-family Prime contraction, including every\naccepted atom's admitted domain and realisation-projection mapping; and\nexplicit deferred, excluded and unresolved functionality.\n\nThe Ontology may be a boundary-bounded slice of a wider accepted product\nOntology. The slice cites its parent and preserves parent identity,\nrelationship, lifecycle, authority and function law. Copying selected rows\ninto a new local model without that derivation creates another truth\nsurface (P6, P3).\n\n","coordinates":{"lines":[1312,1354]},"encoding":"utf8","parent_sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220","selected_sha256":"4dd74835bab9775209a769aa0b59378bbb579294e528c56ba001600da829fd76","size_bytes":2437,"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1312,1354]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"}}},"schema":"a_c.fd-support.response/1","status":"ok"}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KHXeax9EEd8KtJQ3pxBcYD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAzCep9xtcq4ytk4MJNW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RoepHui2BVo94T5Lm5RcAY","name":"mcp__ac_review__source_read","input":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-996","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[996,1052]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6057,"cache_read_input_tokens":160579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6057},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"6575ac45-732b-43bd-8b05-a8b40382dbbe","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:24.777Z","request_id":"req_011CgAzCeYYXNXcPC22F71q4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RoepHui2BVo94T5Lm5RcAY":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-996","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[996,1052]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RoepHui2BVo94T5Lm5RcAY","display_name":"Source Read","server_display_name":"a_c.graphify.fd-support","tool_name":"source.read"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RoepHui2BVo94T5Lm5RcAY","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:6cf83ddaa3aaea3d114cf1ae7ee594499e510400c2d037b24d977eb95f4c9336\",\"sha256\":\"6cf83ddaa3aaea3d114cf1ae7ee594499e510400c2d037b24d977eb95f4c9336\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-996\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=fad69eff79f363e0908abf5066dc607119588f686ec4cb6e01e7ff73b3273cd0]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"### Prime semantic atoms and admitted domain\\n\\nA Prime semantic atom is one irreducible design relation: an atomic\\nfunction family, identity, authority transition, lifecycle relation,\\ntopology, effect, closure law or composition law that cannot be expressed\\nhonestly by composing other accepted atoms while preserving the same\\nmeaning (P3, P1; rc.2 #prime-semantic-atoms-and-universal-domain).\\n\\nPrime is decided at semantic-design altitude. A file, source function,\\nclass, schema record, public operation, diagram, module or owner category\\nis not a Prime atom merely because it is small, typed, separately named or\\nindependently testable. One atom may project into several realisation\\nunits, and one realisation unit may implement several atoms, provided the\\nmapping is explicit and introduces no rival meaning or authority. An\\natomic function is not proven Prime until the complete candidate family has\\nbeen contracted (whole-family Prime contraction, below; P1, P6).\\n\\nEvery accepted Prime atom declares the admitted domain over which its law\\nholds, and within that domain it governs every admitted instance. A later\\nuse case composes or parameterises the atom; it does not fork, shadow,\\nspecialise or reinterpret the atom locally. An out-of-domain case may\\njustify proposing another candidate atom for design admission. An\\nin-domain counterexample falsifies the shared atom and re-enters at that\\natom's design boundary rather than authorising a feature-local replacement\\n(P3, P6). An atom without a declared admitted domain remains a candidate\\natom; it cannot supply Prime acceptance or Prime-conformance to a\\nrealisation projection.\\n\\n### Whole-family Prime contraction\\n\\nPrime review evaluates the complete candidate semantic-atom family and\\nevery proposed realisation projection, not only functions, carriers or\\neach atom in isolation. The family includes every proposed identity,\\nauthority transition, lifecycle relation, topology, function, composition\\nlaw, effect and closure law. A function-and-carrier-only pass does not\\nestablish whole-family Prime (P3, P2;\\nrc.2 #whole-family-prime-contraction). Before promotion, review asks:\\n\\n- can several proposed operations be typed parameters of one atomic\\n  function?\\n- are several commands lifecycle transitions over one entity?\\n- are several reads projections over one source truth?\\n- are several implementations applications of one higher-order function?\\n- does the candidate introduce independent identity, authority, effect,\\n  lifecycle, reuse or public pattern-match semantics?\\n- has every candidate atom kind entered the same contraction?\\n- does every realisation unit map explicitly to its accepted atom or\\n  atoms?\\n- does any realisation projection introduce, duplicate or reinterpret\\n  meaning or authority?\\n\\nA family of individually plausible functions is not Prime when one\\nparameterised template plus closed variants carries the same meaning and\\nauthority. Recurrence is contracted during design when visible; the\\npost-implementation recurrence rule (§10) is not a substitute for this\\nprior pass (P2).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[996,1052]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"563caa6ee8431473a2e36cb480cd1305d16fb06bff43242dda4666f271cae7a5\",\"size_bytes\":3066,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[996,1052]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"f5250319-7f60-45fe-9e75-f26d918501e6","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:24.877Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:6cf83ddaa3aaea3d114cf1ae7ee594499e510400c2d037b24d977eb95f4c9336\",\"sha256\":\"6cf83ddaa3aaea3d114cf1ae7ee594499e510400c2d037b24d977eb95f4c9336\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-996\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=fad69eff79f363e0908abf5066dc607119588f686ec4cb6e01e7ff73b3273cd0]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"### Prime semantic atoms and admitted domain\\n\\nA Prime semantic atom is one irreducible design relation: an atomic\\nfunction family, identity, authority transition, lifecycle relation,\\ntopology, effect, closure law or composition law that cannot be expressed\\nhonestly by composing other accepted atoms while preserving the same\\nmeaning (P3, P1; rc.2 #prime-semantic-atoms-and-universal-domain).\\n\\nPrime is decided at semantic-design altitude. A file, source function,\\nclass, schema record, public operation, diagram, module or owner category\\nis not a Prime atom merely because it is small, typed, separately named or\\nindependently testable. One atom may project into several realisation\\nunits, and one realisation unit may implement several atoms, provided the\\nmapping is explicit and introduces no rival meaning or authority. An\\natomic function is not proven Prime until the complete candidate family has\\nbeen contracted (whole-family Prime contraction, below; P1, P6).\\n\\nEvery accepted Prime atom declares the admitted domain over which its law\\nholds, and within that domain it governs every admitted instance. A later\\nuse case composes or parameterises the atom; it does not fork, shadow,\\nspecialise or reinterpret the atom locally. An out-of-domain case may\\njustify proposing another candidate atom for design admission. An\\nin-domain counterexample falsifies the shared atom and re-enters at that\\natom's design boundary rather than authorising a feature-local replacement\\n(P3, P6). An atom without a declared admitted domain remains a candidate\\natom; it cannot supply Prime acceptance or Prime-conformance to a\\nrealisation projection.\\n\\n### Whole-family Prime contraction\\n\\nPrime review evaluates the complete candidate semantic-atom family and\\nevery proposed realisation projection, not only functions, carriers or\\neach atom in isolation. The family includes every proposed identity,\\nauthority transition, lifecycle relation, topology, function, composition\\nlaw, effect and closure law. A function-and-carrier-only pass does not\\nestablish whole-family Prime (P3, P2;\\nrc.2 #whole-family-prime-contraction). Before promotion, review asks:\\n\\n- can several proposed operations be typed parameters of one atomic\\n  function?\\n- are several commands lifecycle transitions over one entity?\\n- are several reads projections over one source truth?\\n- are several implementations applications of one higher-order function?\\n- does the candidate introduce independent identity, authority, effect,\\n  lifecycle, reuse or public pattern-match semantics?\\n- has every candidate atom kind entered the same contraction?\\n- does every realisation unit map explicitly to its accepted atom or\\n  atoms?\\n- does any realisation projection introduce, duplicate or reinterpret\\n  meaning or authority?\\n\\nA family of individually plausible functions is not Prime when one\\nparameterised template plus closed variants carries the same meaning and\\nauthority. Recurrence is contracted during design when visible; the\\npost-implementation recurrence rule (§10) is not a substitute for this\\nprior pass (P2).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[996,1052]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"563caa6ee8431473a2e36cb480cd1305d16fb06bff43242dda4666f271cae7a5\",\"size_bytes\":3066,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[996,1052]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}","structuredContent":{"evidence":{"id":"sha256:6cf83ddaa3aaea3d114cf1ae7ee594499e510400c2d037b24d977eb95f4c9336","sha256":"6cf83ddaa3aaea3d114cf1ae7ee594499e510400c2d037b24d977eb95f4c9336"},"id":"p2-read-dm-996","operation":"source.read","operation_outcome":{"cause":"declared output available","core_value":"satisfied","evidence":[],"limits":["output availability only; no semantic acceptance"],"predicate":"B(source.read)[request_sha256=fad69eff79f363e0908abf5066dc607119588f686ec4cb6e01e7ff73b3273cd0]: declared output availability","source_parent":{"clause":"results","member":"FOUNDATIONS.md","sha256":"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f"},"value":"returned"},"refusal":null,"result":{"artifact":null,"value":{"acquisition":{},"content":"### Prime semantic atoms and admitted domain\n\nA Prime semantic atom is one irreducible design relation: an atomic\nfunction family, identity, authority transition, lifecycle relation,\ntopology, effect, closure law or composition law that cannot be expressed\nhonestly by composing other accepted atoms while preserving the same\nmeaning (P3, P1; rc.2 #prime-semantic-atoms-and-universal-domain).\n\nPrime is decided at semantic-design altitude. A file, source function,\nclass, schema record, public operation, diagram, module or owner category\nis not a Prime atom merely because it is small, typed, separately named or\nindependently testable. One atom may project into several realisation\nunits, and one realisation unit may implement several atoms, provided the\nmapping is explicit and introduces no rival meaning or authority. An\natomic function is not proven Prime until the complete candidate family has\nbeen contracted (whole-family Prime contraction, below; P1, P6).\n\nEvery accepted Prime atom declares the admitted domain over which its law\nholds, and within that domain it governs every admitted instance. A later\nuse case composes or parameterises the atom; it does not fork, shadow,\nspecialise or reinterpret the atom locally. An out-of-domain case may\njustify proposing another candidate atom for design admission. An\nin-domain counterexample falsifies the shared atom and re-enters at that\natom's design boundary rather than authorising a feature-local replacement\n(P3, P6). An atom without a declared admitted domain remains a candidate\natom; it cannot supply Prime acceptance or Prime-conformance to a\nrealisation projection.\n\n### Whole-family Prime contraction\n\nPrime review evaluates the complete candidate semantic-atom family and\nevery proposed realisation projection, not only functions, carriers or\neach atom in isolation. The family includes every proposed identity,\nauthority transition, lifecycle relation, topology, function, composition\nlaw, effect and closure law. A function-and-carrier-only pass does not\nestablish whole-family Prime (P3, P2;\nrc.2 #whole-family-prime-contraction). Before promotion, review asks:\n\n- can several proposed operations be typed parameters of one atomic\n  function?\n- are several commands lifecycle transitions over one entity?\n- are several reads projections over one source truth?\n- are several implementations applications of one higher-order function?\n- does the candidate introduce independent identity, authority, effect,\n  lifecycle, reuse or public pattern-match semantics?\n- has every candidate atom kind entered the same contraction?\n- does every realisation unit map explicitly to its accepted atom or\n  atoms?\n- does any realisation projection introduce, duplicate or reinterpret\n  meaning or authority?\n\nA family of individually plausible functions is not Prime when one\nparameterised template plus closed variants carries the same meaning and\nauthority. Recurrence is contracted during design when visible; the\npost-implementation recurrence rule (§10) is not a substitute for this\nprior pass (P2).\n\n","coordinates":{"lines":[996,1052]},"encoding":"utf8","parent_sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220","selected_sha256":"563caa6ee8431473a2e36cb480cd1305d16fb06bff43242dda4666f271cae7a5","size_bytes":3066,"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[996,1052]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"}}},"schema":"a_c.fd-support.response/1","status":"ok"}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RoepHui2BVo94T5Lm5RcAY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAzCep9xtcq4ytk4MJNW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tf1pHet729nyghNLLRHbYK","name":"mcp__ac_review__source_read","input":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-1101","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1101,1153]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6057,"cache_read_input_tokens":160579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6057},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"f9421f1b-ad85-449e-9462-1d5f58cd2e07","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:27.390Z","request_id":"req_011CgAzCeYYXNXcPC22F71q4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Tf1pHet729nyghNLLRHbYK":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-1101","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1101,1153]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Tf1pHet729nyghNLLRHbYK","display_name":"Source Read","server_display_name":"a_c.graphify.fd-support","tool_name":"source.read"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tf1pHet729nyghNLLRHbYK","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:f3678de2a33c4b2919e351e2af9f6ead1c389bc298564740149df3f8762d25b0\",\"sha256\":\"f3678de2a33c4b2919e351e2af9f6ead1c389bc298564740149df3f8762d25b0\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-1101\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=33e101523f90772943e15c1039e1be112dc2b90860a30852894abe79981b9c1b]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 6. Prime law\\n\\nUsing the logically complete candidate Ontology, derive the Prime semantic\\natoms before proposing top-level realisation units. Whole-family Prime is\\npart of the evidence used to accept the Ontology basis. Target design,\\nIACS, modules, source functions, schemas and tests project those accepted\\natoms; they do not originate Prime meaning. Prime applies both to each\\nsemantic atom and to the candidate family as a whole (P3, P2;\\nrc.2 #5-prime-law).\\n\\nPrime-conformance applies to these realisation projections: functions,\\nclasses, carrier types, top-level schema records and modules. Typed\\nclosure by itself is not sufficient reason to create a new top-level type.\\nThe hard vocabulary of this law (P1):\\n\\n- Irreducible Architectural Carrier Set (IACS): the smallest lawful\\n  top-level carrier family required for one boundary;\\n- Subordinate Payload: payload detail that exists inside that boundary but\\n  is not top-level by default;\\n- Promotion Test: the explicit test a Subordinate Payload passes before it\\n  becomes a top-level type; and\\n- Boundary Inflation: the defect where subordinate payload detail is\\n  promoted into extra peer types without irreducible need.\\n\\nA top-level realisation unit is Prime-conformant only when it projects one\\nor more accepted Prime atoms without introducing, duplicating or\\nreinterpreting semantic authority. A topological or effect boundary is\\nfirst derived and accepted as a Prime atom. Source-code size and\\none-to-one correspondence with an atom are immaterial (P3, P6).\\n\\nTypical lawful reasons to introduce a new top-level realisation unit are a\\nnew feature topology or branch in the admitted work graph, a new typed\\ncarrier transformation, a new effect boundary, a new validation or\\nadmission boundary, and a new projection boundary. A realisation unit is\\nnot Prime-conformant when it exists only to: shorten a call site without\\ncreating a new semantic boundary; aggregate unrelated behaviour for\\nconvenience; preserve an old interface behind a new name; shuttle data\\nthrough one more wrapper layer; hide mutation, fallback or orchestration\\nthat should remain explicit; name one more payload variation that carries\\nno independent authority; turn internal record detail into public schema\\nsurface without an irreducible boundary; mirror another top-level carrier\\nwith only small field variation; or respond to typing discomfort by\\nmultiplying peer classes instead of closing one governing carrier family\\n(P3, P2).\\n\\nThis is Occam's razor for design modules: prefer composition over\\nconvenience extraction; prefer existing lawful functions over new\\nwrappers; prefer fewer, sharper boundaries over many vague helper\\nfunctions; prefer fewer, sharper carrier families over many payload-shaped\\npeer types. The purpose of the Prime law is not to minimise line count; it\\nis to stop semantic fragmentation and helper sprawl from becoming\\narchitecture (P2).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[1101,1153]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"1bf5503937a870e7bed0f2c0537382786f5a25e97956abb81961cd059a4deb68\",\"size_bytes\":2901,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[1101,1153]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"58342b7e-330e-423f-9356-7504cedb45a5","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:27.401Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:f3678de2a33c4b2919e351e2af9f6ead1c389bc298564740149df3f8762d25b0\",\"sha256\":\"f3678de2a33c4b2919e351e2af9f6ead1c389bc298564740149df3f8762d25b0\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-1101\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=33e101523f90772943e15c1039e1be112dc2b90860a30852894abe79981b9c1b]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 6. Prime law\\n\\nUsing the logically complete candidate Ontology, derive the Prime semantic\\natoms before proposing top-level realisation units. Whole-family Prime is\\npart of the evidence used to accept the Ontology basis. Target design,\\nIACS, modules, source functions, schemas and tests project those accepted\\natoms; they do not originate Prime meaning. Prime applies both to each\\nsemantic atom and to the candidate family as a whole (P3, P2;\\nrc.2 #5-prime-law).\\n\\nPrime-conformance applies to these realisation projections: functions,\\nclasses, carrier types, top-level schema records and modules. Typed\\nclosure by itself is not sufficient reason to create a new top-level type.\\nThe hard vocabulary of this law (P1):\\n\\n- Irreducible Architectural Carrier Set (IACS): the smallest lawful\\n  top-level carrier family required for one boundary;\\n- Subordinate Payload: payload detail that exists inside that boundary but\\n  is not top-level by default;\\n- Promotion Test: the explicit test a Subordinate Payload passes before it\\n  becomes a top-level type; and\\n- Boundary Inflation: the defect where subordinate payload detail is\\n  promoted into extra peer types without irreducible need.\\n\\nA top-level realisation unit is Prime-conformant only when it projects one\\nor more accepted Prime atoms without introducing, duplicating or\\nreinterpreting semantic authority. A topological or effect boundary is\\nfirst derived and accepted as a Prime atom. Source-code size and\\none-to-one correspondence with an atom are immaterial (P3, P6).\\n\\nTypical lawful reasons to introduce a new top-level realisation unit are a\\nnew feature topology or branch in the admitted work graph, a new typed\\ncarrier transformation, a new effect boundary, a new validation or\\nadmission boundary, and a new projection boundary. A realisation unit is\\nnot Prime-conformant when it exists only to: shorten a call site without\\ncreating a new semantic boundary; aggregate unrelated behaviour for\\nconvenience; preserve an old interface behind a new name; shuttle data\\nthrough one more wrapper layer; hide mutation, fallback or orchestration\\nthat should remain explicit; name one more payload variation that carries\\nno independent authority; turn internal record detail into public schema\\nsurface without an irreducible boundary; mirror another top-level carrier\\nwith only small field variation; or respond to typing discomfort by\\nmultiplying peer classes instead of closing one governing carrier family\\n(P3, P2).\\n\\nThis is Occam's razor for design modules: prefer composition over\\nconvenience extraction; prefer existing lawful functions over new\\nwrappers; prefer fewer, sharper boundaries over many vague helper\\nfunctions; prefer fewer, sharper carrier families over many payload-shaped\\npeer types. The purpose of the Prime law is not to minimise line count; it\\nis to stop semantic fragmentation and helper sprawl from becoming\\narchitecture (P2).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[1101,1153]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"1bf5503937a870e7bed0f2c0537382786f5a25e97956abb81961cd059a4deb68\",\"size_bytes\":2901,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[1101,1153]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}","structuredContent":{"evidence":{"id":"sha256:f3678de2a33c4b2919e351e2af9f6ead1c389bc298564740149df3f8762d25b0","sha256":"f3678de2a33c4b2919e351e2af9f6ead1c389bc298564740149df3f8762d25b0"},"id":"p2-read-dm-1101","operation":"source.read","operation_outcome":{"cause":"declared output available","core_value":"satisfied","evidence":[],"limits":["output availability only; no semantic acceptance"],"predicate":"B(source.read)[request_sha256=33e101523f90772943e15c1039e1be112dc2b90860a30852894abe79981b9c1b]: declared output availability","source_parent":{"clause":"results","member":"FOUNDATIONS.md","sha256":"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f"},"value":"returned"},"refusal":null,"result":{"artifact":null,"value":{"acquisition":{},"content":"## 6. Prime law\n\nUsing the logically complete candidate Ontology, derive the Prime semantic\natoms before proposing top-level realisation units. Whole-family Prime is\npart of the evidence used to accept the Ontology basis. Target design,\nIACS, modules, source functions, schemas and tests project those accepted\natoms; they do not originate Prime meaning. Prime applies both to each\nsemantic atom and to the candidate family as a whole (P3, P2;\nrc.2 #5-prime-law).\n\nPrime-conformance applies to these realisation projections: functions,\nclasses, carrier types, top-level schema records and modules. Typed\nclosure by itself is not sufficient reason to create a new top-level type.\nThe hard vocabulary of this law (P1):\n\n- Irreducible Architectural Carrier Set (IACS): the smallest lawful\n  top-level carrier family required for one boundary;\n- Subordinate Payload: payload detail that exists inside that boundary but\n  is not top-level by default;\n- Promotion Test: the explicit test a Subordinate Payload passes before it\n  becomes a top-level type; and\n- Boundary Inflation: the defect where subordinate payload detail is\n  promoted into extra peer types without irreducible need.\n\nA top-level realisation unit is Prime-conformant only when it projects one\nor more accepted Prime atoms without introducing, duplicating or\nreinterpreting semantic authority. A topological or effect boundary is\nfirst derived and accepted as a Prime atom. Source-code size and\none-to-one correspondence with an atom are immaterial (P3, P6).\n\nTypical lawful reasons to introduce a new top-level realisation unit are a\nnew feature topology or branch in the admitted work graph, a new typed\ncarrier transformation, a new effect boundary, a new validation or\nadmission boundary, and a new projection boundary. A realisation unit is\nnot Prime-conformant when it exists only to: shorten a call site without\ncreating a new semantic boundary; aggregate unrelated behaviour for\nconvenience; preserve an old interface behind a new name; shuttle data\nthrough one more wrapper layer; hide mutation, fallback or orchestration\nthat should remain explicit; name one more payload variation that carries\nno independent authority; turn internal record detail into public schema\nsurface without an irreducible boundary; mirror another top-level carrier\nwith only small field variation; or respond to typing discomfort by\nmultiplying peer classes instead of closing one governing carrier family\n(P3, P2).\n\nThis is Occam's razor for design modules: prefer composition over\nconvenience extraction; prefer existing lawful functions over new\nwrappers; prefer fewer, sharper boundaries over many vague helper\nfunctions; prefer fewer, sharper carrier families over many payload-shaped\npeer types. The purpose of the Prime law is not to minimise line count; it\nis to stop semantic fragmentation and helper sprawl from becoming\narchitecture (P2).\n\n","coordinates":{"lines":[1101,1153]},"encoding":"utf8","parent_sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220","selected_sha256":"1bf5503937a870e7bed0f2c0537382786f5a25e97956abb81961cd059a4deb68","size_bytes":2901,"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1101,1153]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"}}},"schema":"a_c.fd-support.response/1","status":"ok"}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Tf1pHet729nyghNLLRHbYK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAzCep9xtcq4ytk4MJNW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VutcYFUFKAa5M6WBMSGFZv","name":"mcp__ac_review__source_read","input":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-1479","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1479,1547]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6057,"cache_read_input_tokens":160579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6057},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"63da0e77-7e19-42c1-86a7-47ba967fb9b4","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:30.162Z","request_id":"req_011CgAzCeYYXNXcPC22F71q4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VutcYFUFKAa5M6WBMSGFZv":{"schema":"a_c.fd-support.request/1","id":"p2-read-dm-1479","operation":"source.read","arguments":{"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1479,1547]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"},"encoding":"utf8","max_content_bytes":8192}}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VutcYFUFKAa5M6WBMSGFZv","display_name":"Source Read","server_display_name":"a_c.graphify.fd-support","tool_name":"source.read"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VutcYFUFKAa5M6WBMSGFZv","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:4516354fdab0869a650efcdacae513f806c281f280c757fe526e307f4133cc1b\",\"sha256\":\"4516354fdab0869a650efcdacae513f806c281f280c757fe526e307f4133cc1b\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-1479\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=678c291f0cbed78796d694065db928a4f39743d9c8194821653e6e6cc5922219]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"### Cross-view axiom evaluation\\n\\nThe design pack evaluates every applicable product, graph, language,\\nruntime, handler and module axiom with one of `pass`, `fail` or\\n`not_applicable` with a reason (P1, P6; rc.2 #cross-view-axiom-evaluation).\\nThe minimum evaluation columns are:\\n\\n| Axiom | Ontology evidence | Authority | Domain evidence | Sequence evidence | State evidence | Native enforcement | Admission/compiler enforcement | Verdict | Gap owner |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n\\nThe cross-view evaluation proves that: every element in each view derives\\nfrom the accepted Ontology; every discovered functionality row has a\\nderived, deferred, excluded or gap disposition; every sequence participant\\nand lifecycle carrier exists in the domain model; every sequence message\\nhas a declared semantic or effect boundary; every lifecycle transition is\\nderivable from admitted truth; every function and transition has an\\nexplicit authority path and composition does not widen that authority\\nimplicitly; every public operation is a projection of an Ontology function\\nrather than an independently authored semantic peer; raw probabilistic\\noutput cannot transition directly to accepted or closed; handlers and\\nplugins own interiors only when the runtime owns admission, events,\\ncontinuation and closure; native types enforce locally decidable\\nrelations; and admission or semantic compilation owns global references,\\ncompleteness and realisation gaps (P1, P6, P5).\\n\\nFor graph-native work, every batch, retry, recursion or nested workflow is\\nvisible through the product's declared graph or compute algebra. A\\nrelied-on `semantic_not_realized` constructor is a blocking gap; it may\\nnot be replaced by imperative glue in a plugin, shell, service, script or\\ntest harness (P6, P5).\\n\\nAxiom verdicts. The three verdicts are a refinement of the core result\\nalgebra ([FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results)). They answer\\nwhat the design pack concludes about one axiom for the active boundary,\\nand each maps to exactly one core value for the predicate \\\"on the exact\\nOntology and design basis, the boundary's design meets the axiom, or the\\naxiom does not apply to the boundary\\\"; the value travels with that\\npredicate and with the verdict, whose meaning stays here (P5, P6). This\\nprofile declares that predicate as a disjunction that includes\\nnon-application, the case that\\n[ACTIVATION §1 Applicability](ACTIVATION.md#applicability), Exclusion and\\nthe core, permits and bounds. That paragraph decides what follows for the\\nverdicts: `not_applicable` rests on a false applicability premise, an\\nunresolved premise supports no `not_applicable`, and the `satisfied` of a\\n`not_applicable` verdict counts toward this disjunction only, never toward\\nthe predicate that the design meets the axiom:\\n\\n| Verdict | Ground | Core value |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `pass` | the cited Ontology, view and enforcement evidence establishes that the design meets the axiom | `satisfied` |\\n| `not_applicable` | the axiom's applicability premise is false for the active boundary on basis-bound facts, and the reason names those facts and the authority whose scope declaration excludes the axiom ([ACTIVATION §1 Applicability](ACTIVATION.md#applicability)) | `satisfied` |\\n| `fail` | the axiom applies, and the evidence names an element, message, transition, authority path or enforcement that violates it; that is the counterexample | `falsified` |\\n\\nThe verdict, recorded with its value, keeps the distinction between not\\napplying and meeting the axiom (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results, Recording). An evaluated axiom that reaches none of the three\\nverdicts records in the Verdict column the core value its cause selects,\\nwith what would decide it: `indeterminate` where the admissible evidence\\ncannot decide the predicate, as where the applicability premise is\\nunresolved and the design does not meet the axiom; `out_of_frame` where\\nthe evaluation needs a relation or evaluator capability that the activated\\nframe does not declare; `invalid_basis` where the Ontology or design basis\\ncannot support it. An applicable axiom that the pack did not evaluate has\\nno result (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results, Not performed). None of these is\\n`pass`. The mapping and the direct core values are new in this corpus.\\nPurpose and material relation: a design gate that combines axiom verdicts\\nwith other results reads them in the one core, so an excluded axiom is\\nnever read as conforming or as failing, and an undecided or unevaluated\\naxiom is never read as a pass or a fail (P5, P6).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[1479,1547]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"5787323dd25f8b0dd573b4a9174ba465a32c4b5775d480ba916be9adb0a423e4\",\"size_bytes\":4528,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[1479,1547]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d70f2d0-ea95-44de-98cd-d06e9ca7170b","uuid":"b9101d5d-42ac-4573-bd6b-9a921f36db7b","timestamp":"2026-10-19T04:45:30.519Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:4516354fdab0869a650efcdacae513f806c281f280c757fe526e307f4133cc1b\",\"sha256\":\"4516354fdab0869a650efcdacae513f806c281f280c757fe526e307f4133cc1b\"},\"id\":\"p2-read-dm-1479\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=678c291f0cbed78796d694065db928a4f39743d9c8194821653e6e6cc5922219]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"### Cross-view axiom evaluation\\n\\nThe design pack evaluates every applicable product, graph, language,\\nruntime, handler and module axiom with one of `pass`, `fail` or\\n`not_applicable` with a reason (P1, P6; rc.2 #cross-view-axiom-evaluation).\\nThe minimum evaluation columns are:\\n\\n| Axiom | Ontology evidence | Authority | Domain evidence | Sequence evidence | State evidence | Native enforcement | Admission/compiler enforcement | Verdict | Gap owner |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n\\nThe cross-view evaluation proves that: every element in each view derives\\nfrom the accepted Ontology; every discovered functionality row has a\\nderived, deferred, excluded or gap disposition; every sequence participant\\nand lifecycle carrier exists in the domain model; every sequence message\\nhas a declared semantic or effect boundary; every lifecycle transition is\\nderivable from admitted truth; every function and transition has an\\nexplicit authority path and composition does not widen that authority\\nimplicitly; every public operation is a projection of an Ontology function\\nrather than an independently authored semantic peer; raw probabilistic\\noutput cannot transition directly to accepted or closed; handlers and\\nplugins own interiors only when the runtime owns admission, events,\\ncontinuation and closure; native types enforce locally decidable\\nrelations; and admission or semantic compilation owns global references,\\ncompleteness and realisation gaps (P1, P6, P5).\\n\\nFor graph-native work, every batch, retry, recursion or nested workflow is\\nvisible through the product's declared graph or compute algebra. A\\nrelied-on `semantic_not_realized` constructor is a blocking gap; it may\\nnot be replaced by imperative glue in a plugin, shell, service, script or\\ntest harness (P6, P5).\\n\\nAxiom verdicts. The three verdicts are a refinement of the core result\\nalgebra ([FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results)). They answer\\nwhat the design pack concludes about one axiom for the active boundary,\\nand each maps to exactly one core value for the predicate \\\"on the exact\\nOntology and design basis, the boundary's design meets the axiom, or the\\naxiom does not apply to the boundary\\\"; the value travels with that\\npredicate and with the verdict, whose meaning stays here (P5, P6). This\\nprofile declares that predicate as a disjunction that includes\\nnon-application, the case that\\n[ACTIVATION §1 Applicability](ACTIVATION.md#applicability), Exclusion and\\nthe core, permits and bounds. That paragraph decides what follows for the\\nverdicts: `not_applicable` rests on a false applicability premise, an\\nunresolved premise supports no `not_applicable`, and the `satisfied` of a\\n`not_applicable` verdict counts toward this disjunction only, never toward\\nthe predicate that the design meets the axiom:\\n\\n| Verdict | Ground | Core value |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `pass` | the cited Ontology, view and enforcement evidence establishes that the design meets the axiom | `satisfied` |\\n| `not_applicable` | the axiom's applicability premise is false for the active boundary on basis-bound facts, and the reason names those facts and the authority whose scope declaration excludes the axiom ([ACTIVATION §1 Applicability](ACTIVATION.md#applicability)) | `satisfied` |\\n| `fail` | the axiom applies, and the evidence names an element, message, transition, authority path or enforcement that violates it; that is the counterexample | `falsified` |\\n\\nThe verdict, recorded with its value, keeps the distinction between not\\napplying and meeting the axiom (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results, Recording). An evaluated axiom that reaches none of the three\\nverdicts records in the Verdict column the core value its cause selects,\\nwith what would decide it: `indeterminate` where the admissible evidence\\ncannot decide the predicate, as where the applicability premise is\\nunresolved and the design does not meet the axiom; `out_of_frame` where\\nthe evaluation needs a relation or evaluator capability that the activated\\nframe does not declare; `invalid_basis` where the Ontology or design basis\\ncannot support it. An applicable axiom that the pack did not evaluate has\\nno result (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results, Not performed). None of these is\\n`pass`. The mapping and the direct core values are new in this corpus.\\nPurpose and material relation: a design gate that combines axiom verdicts\\nwith other results reads them in the one core, so an excluded axiom is\\nnever read as conforming or as failing, and an undecided or unevaluated\\naxiom is never read as a pass or a fail (P5, P6).\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[1479,1547]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\",\"selected_sha256\":\"5787323dd25f8b0dd573b4a9174ba465a32c4b5775d480ba916be9adb0a423e4\",\"size_bytes\":4528,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"DESIGN_MODULE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[1479,1547]},\"sha256\":\"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}","structuredContent":{"evidence":{"id":"sha256:4516354fdab0869a650efcdacae513f806c281f280c757fe526e307f4133cc1b","sha256":"4516354fdab0869a650efcdacae513f806c281f280c757fe526e307f4133cc1b"},"id":"p2-read-dm-1479","operation":"source.read","operation_outcome":{"cause":"declared output available","core_value":"satisfied","evidence":[],"limits":["output availability only; no semantic acceptance"],"predicate":"B(source.read)[request_sha256=678c291f0cbed78796d694065db928a4f39743d9c8194821653e6e6cc5922219]: declared output availability","source_parent":{"clause":"results","member":"FOUNDATIONS.md","sha256":"fd2d895f90eb39bda0950c1c7ff02057ef5d8ae73fcbc5b3203f7e99081d6e3f"},"value":"returned"},"refusal":null,"result":{"artifact":null,"value":{"acquisition":{},"content":"### Cross-view axiom evaluation\n\nThe design pack evaluates every applicable product, graph, language,\nruntime, handler and module axiom with one of `pass`, `fail` or\n`not_applicable` with a reason (P1, P6; rc.2 #cross-view-axiom-evaluation).\nThe minimum evaluation columns are:\n\n| Axiom | Ontology evidence | Authority | Domain evidence | Sequence evidence | State evidence | Native enforcement | Admission/compiler enforcement | Verdict | Gap owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nThe cross-view evaluation proves that: every element in each view derives\nfrom the accepted Ontology; every discovered functionality row has a\nderived, deferred, excluded or gap disposition; every sequence participant\nand lifecycle carrier exists in the domain model; every sequence message\nhas a declared semantic or effect boundary; every lifecycle transition is\nderivable from admitted truth; every function and transition has an\nexplicit authority path and composition does not widen that authority\nimplicitly; every public operation is a projection of an Ontology function\nrather than an independently authored semantic peer; raw probabilistic\noutput cannot transition directly to accepted or closed; handlers and\nplugins own interiors only when the runtime owns admission, events,\ncontinuation and closure; native types enforce locally decidable\nrelations; and admission or semantic compilation owns global references,\ncompleteness and realisation gaps (P1, P6, P5).\n\nFor graph-native work, every batch, retry, recursion or nested workflow is\nvisible through the product's declared graph or compute algebra. A\nrelied-on `semantic_not_realized` constructor is a blocking gap; it may\nnot be replaced by imperative glue in a plugin, shell, service, script or\ntest harness (P6, P5).\n\nAxiom verdicts. The three verdicts are a refinement of the core result\nalgebra ([FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results)). They answer\nwhat the design pack concludes about one axiom for the active boundary,\nand each maps to exactly one core value for the predicate \"on the exact\nOntology and design basis, the boundary's design meets the axiom, or the\naxiom does not apply to the boundary\"; the value travels with that\npredicate and with the verdict, whose meaning stays here (P5, P6). This\nprofile declares that predicate as a disjunction that includes\nnon-application, the case that\n[ACTIVATION §1 Applicability](ACTIVATION.md#applicability), Exclusion and\nthe core, permits and bounds. That paragraph decides what follows for the\nverdicts: `not_applicable` rests on a false applicability premise, an\nunresolved premise supports no `not_applicable`, and the `satisfied` of a\n`not_applicable` verdict counts toward this disjunction only, never toward\nthe predicate that the design meets the axiom:\n\n| Verdict | Ground | Core value |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pass` | the cited Ontology, view and enforcement evidence establishes that the design meets the axiom | `satisfied` |\n| `not_applicable` | the axiom's applicability premise is false for the active boundary on basis-bound facts, and the reason names those facts and the authority whose scope declaration excludes the axiom ([ACTIVATION §1 Applicability](ACTIVATION.md#applicability)) | `satisfied` |\n| `fail` | the axiom applies, and the evidence names an element, message, transition, authority path or enforcement that violates it; that is the counterexample | `falsified` |\n\nThe verdict, recorded with its value, keeps the distinction between not\napplying and meeting the axiom (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results, Recording). An evaluated axiom that reaches none of the three\nverdicts records in the Verdict column the core value its cause selects,\nwith what would decide it: `indeterminate` where the admissible evidence\ncannot decide the predicate, as where the applicability premise is\nunresolved and the design does not meet the axiom; `out_of_frame` where\nthe evaluation needs a relation or evaluator capability that the activated\nframe does not declare; `invalid_basis` where the Ontology or design basis\ncannot support it. An applicable axiom that the pack did not evaluate has\nno result (FOUNDATIONS §4 Results, Not performed). None of these is\n`pass`. The mapping and the direct core values are new in this corpus.\nPurpose and material relation: a design gate that combines axiom verdicts\nwith other results reads them in the one core, so an excluded axiom is\nnever read as conforming or as failing, and an undecided or unevaluated\naxiom is never read as a pass or a fail (P5, P6).\n\n","coordinates":{"lines":[1479,1547]},"encoding":"utf8","parent_sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220","selected_sha256":"5787323dd25f8b0dd573b4a9174ba465a32c4b5775d480ba916be9adb0a423e4","size_bytes":4528,"source":{"engine":"workspace-regular-file","object":null,"path":"DESIGN_MODULE.md","root":"method","selector":{"lines":[1479,1547]},"sha256":"9e2642d51d0b024a448fe7910dcb323fb3f3bb2a003ad33c27c39a5dda625220"}}},"schema":"a_c.fd-support.response/1","status":"ok"}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VutcYFUFKAa5M6WBMSGFZv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
